              "title": "Nurses' Notes",
              "content": "<p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1000:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Liam (8) and Marcus (21) present together. Liam: Mother notes Liam had mosquito bites on his chin 5 days ago; he scratched them persistently. Two days ago, small blisters appeared that burst and formed thick, sticky, yellowish-golden crusts that look like dried honey around his perioral area, nares, and chin. Lesions are weeping and itchy; child rubs face constantly. Vital signs: temp 37.1 °C, HR 92, RR 18. Marcus: 21-year-old college student; reports 4 days of severe burning, tingling, and deep aching pain along the right side of his mid-back and ribs. Yesterday morning, a crop of red bumps and clustered fluid-filled blisters erupted along that exact line. He states: 'Even my shirt brushing against my rib cage feels like fire.' Medical history: had chickenpox at age 4; no history of shingles. Physical exam confirms unilateral band-like vesicular eruption along right T6 dermatome, not crossing midline. Clear fluid in vesicles; no pustules or crusting yet. Nurse prepares infection control isolation and assessment.</span></p>"
