              "id": "nn_1",
              "title": "Nurses' Notes",
              "content": "<p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">0900:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Maya Al-Mansoor, a 28-year-old female, presents for evaluation. History: reports that over the past 2 months she has experienced profound fatigue, intermittent low-grade fevers, and morning stiffness lasting over an hour in both hands and wrists. Last weekend, after attending an outdoor family gathering at the beach, she developed a prominent, burning red rash across her cheeks and the bridge of her nose that has not resolved. Physical examination: facial inspection reveals a confluent, bright red, slightly edematous erythema spanning across bilateral cheeks and connecting over the nasal bridge, with distinct sparing of the nasolabial folds (classic malar distribution). Oral inspection reveals two shallow, painless ulcerations on the hard palate. Musculoskeletal exam: bilateral MCP and PIP joints are tender to palpation with mild synovial puffiness and restricted grip strength; no joint deformity. Integumentary: no silvery plaques on extensor surfaces; no flexural lichenification. Vital signs: temp 37.6 °C, BP 132/84 mmHg, HR 78 bpm. Diagnostic autoantibody serology and urinalysis ordered.</span></p>"
