          "explanation": "The client presents with classic manifestations of acute ischemic stroke: sudden right-sided facial droop and ptosis, right-sided hemiparesis, expressive aphasia, marked hypertension (BP 188/90), and an irregular apical pulse (history of atrial fibrillation). Immediate actions include administering oxygen via nasal cannula to maintain adequate cerebral oxygenation (pulse oximetry is 90% on room air) and inserting a peripheral venous access device (VAD) for emergency fluid management, blood sampling, and potential IV thrombolytic administration. Corticosteroids are not indicated for ischemic stroke. Although hypoglycemia can produce stroke-like focal deficits, the client's blood glucose is 4.2 mmol/L (within acceptable range), so IV 50% dextrose is not indicated. Essential parameters to monitor include frequent neurologic assessments (e.g., NIH Stroke Scale) to evaluate for deterioration or hemorrhagic transformation, and serum glucose levels, as hyperglycemia exacerbates cerebral ischemia.",
